OPEN DECISIONS — resolve before publishing, then delete this section

1. LEGAL ENTITY. "Proximi.io" is used throughout as the licensor. Replace it
   with the registered company name, company number and registered address of
   the entity that actually owns the SDK and contracts with customers. A
   licence granted by a brand name rather than a legal person is weak.

2. GOVERNING LAW AND FORUM (clause 12). Not decided, and deliberately not
   guessed. Pick the jurisdiction of the licensing entity unless there is a
   reason not to. Decide separately whether disputes go to courts or to
   arbitration, and whether a customer's own commercial agreement is allowed to
   override it (clause 12.3 currently says yes).

3. LIABILITY CAP (clause 10.2). "Fees paid in the preceding twelve months" is a
   common figure, not a decision anyone has made here. It is also incoherent
   for a customer with a free or trial tier, who has paid nothing — decide
   whether a floor applies. Confirm the cap is compatible with the cap in the
   standard commercial agreement, since clause 7.2 makes that one prevail.

4. WARRANTY POSTURE (clause 9). Drafted as a full "as is" disclaimer. Confirm
   that is the intended commercial posture, and that it is enforceable in the
   jurisdictions you sell into — in the EU, a business-to-business disclaimer
   this broad is generally allowed, but a customer who resells to consumers may
   push back. Clause 9.3's safety-critical exclusion should be checked against
   what Proximi.io actually tells customers in sales conversations.

5. THIRD-PARTY NOTICES PLACEMENT (clause 8). The GRDB MIT notice is reproduced
   inline here because this LICENSE file is the only legal text the SDK's
   release pipeline currently copies into the distribution repository — there
   is no NOTICES.md on the SDK side, unlike the map package, which ships
   NOTICES.md and ThirdParty-MapLibre-LICENSE.txt alongside its licence. A
   separate NOTICES.md for the SDK would be cleaner and would match the map,
   but it needs a one-line change to scripts/publish-binary-release.sh, which
   was deliberately not made here. Decide which shape you want. See
   docs/licensing-notes.md.

6. TERMINATION AND SHIPPED APPS (clause 11.3). The draft says end users keep
   working copies after termination. That is the customer-friendly reading and
   almost certainly what you want commercially, but it is a choice: confirm it,
   and confirm it does not conflict with the token/service shutoff that the
   commercial agreement presumably allows on termination.

7. CLAUSE 3.4 (COMPETING USE). Drafted broadly. Broad non-compete language in a
   licence can be unenforceable in some jurisdictions and can scare off
   customers who build adjacent products. Decide how wide it should really be —
   in particular whether "anything you learn from it" survives review.

8. CONSISTENCY WITH THE PODSPEC. `Proximiio.podspec` carries a short inline
   licence summary (CocoaPods requires text inline, because the pod's `:http`
   source contains only the xcframework zip). It says "licensed not sold,
   subject to a separate written commercial agreement, see the LICENSE file".
   That stays accurate against this draft; re-check it if clause 7 changes.
